---
import TransparencyPage from '../../components/content/TransparencyPage.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../../data/site.config';

const updated = 'July 25, 2026';
---

<TransparencyPage
  title="AI Use Policy"
  description="How AIViewer’s autonomous editorial system researches, writes, illustrates, checks, and maintains the publication."
  label="Autonomous publishing disclosure"
  heading="AI Use Policy"
  intro="AIViewer is an AI-operated publication. This policy explains exactly what that means, what readers should not assume, and how evidence—not a fictional newsroom—supports our work."
  updated={updated}
  breadcrumb="AI Use Policy"
>
  <h2>An AI-operated publication</h2>
  <p>
    AIViewer’s research, drafting, editing, illustration, coding, and maintenance may be performed substantially by AI agents. The founder sets the mission and owns the publication, but readers should not assume that a human editor has reviewed every sentence before publication. We state the production mode on each new editorial page instead of hiding it behind a generic “editorial team” byline.
  </p>
  <p>
    Autonomy does not lower the evidence standard. “The model produced it” is never an excuse for an invented source, copied passage, unsupported claim, fake test, or misleading recommendation. The public page must show enough source and limitation information for a reader to challenge the work.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable uses</h2>
  <p>Editors and developers may use AI tools to assist with work such as:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>organizing notes, transcripts, or source material;</li>
    <li>suggesting outlines, questions, counterarguments, or plain-language explanations;</li>
    <li>producing a source-backed draft and checking its citations, structure, internal links, and policy requirements;</li>
    <li>copyediting, translation support, metadata, and accessibility text;</li>
    <li>extracting structured fields from verified sources, subject to checking;</li>
    <li>supporting code, data analysis, design exploration, and quality assurance; and</li>
    <li>creating clearly contextualized illustrations or other visual assets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Uses we do not permit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Publishing unverified AI output as fact or disguising materially AI-assisted work as solely human-authored.</li>
    <li>Inventing or guessing facts, statistics, quotations, entities, dates, URLs, test results, or credentials.</li>
    <li>Presenting generated commentary as first-hand product testing.</li>
    <li>Assigning a review score solely because an automated system inferred one from metadata or marketing claims.</li>
    <li>Rewriting another publisher's work to disguise its origin.</li>
    <li>Using automation primarily to create large numbers of search-targeted pages without distinct reader value.</li>
    <li>Creating a fake author, source, testimonial, screenshot, or user experience.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Page-by-page verification requirements</h2>
  <p>
    AI output is never treated as a source. Consequential factual claims are checked against current primary evidence where it exists. A source link must lead to the material it is cited for. Product conclusions must match the access and testing actually performed. When evidence remains uncertain, the page says so or removes the claim. Automated checks can detect missing fields, stale review dates, broken links, and duplicate routes, but they cannot turn weak evidence into a fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Disclosure is the default</h2>
  <p>
    New lessons, signals, foundations, and workflows include a page-level AI-use statement. It identifies whether AI materially performed research, source review, drafting, editing, translation, or image generation. We do not use “human-reviewed,” “expert-reviewed,” or a named reviewer unless that review actually occurred.
  </p>
  <p>
    A named human may occasionally contribute or review a page. That is additional provenance, not a standing claim about the site.
  </p>

  <h2>Generated images and demonstrations</h2>
  <p>
    Editorial cover images may be AI-generated and are presentation, not documentary proof. They must not be presented as a real event, authentic product screenshot, creator thumbnail, or evidence of a test. Genuine product screenshots may be cropped or redacted for privacy, but not altered to misrepresent what the product did.
  </p>

  <h2>Structured and automated pages</h2>
  <p>
    Automation may organize verified facts into a consistent reference format. A structured reference page is not automatically a review, recommendation, or proof of first-hand experience. It should be indexed or monetized only when it offers enough original commentary, curation, analysis, or practical utility to stand on its own for readers.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy and confidential information</h2>
  <p>
    Editors should not place confidential business material, unpublished reader correspondence, account credentials, or unnecessary personal information into third-party AI systems. When a legitimate workflow requires sensitive material, it should be minimized or de-identified and handled under the applicable provider terms and our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Authorship, copyright, and attribution</h2>
  <p>
    AI assistance does not make copied expression original. AIViewer attributes creators and external reporting, respects copyright and licenses, avoids close imitation of a living creator’s distinctive work, and uses official embeds rather than republishing transcripts or copied thumbnails. The publisher is identified as AIViewer; a named human contribution is credited only when it occurred.
  </p>

  <h2>Legacy content and enforcement</h2>
  <p>
    This policy applies to content published or materially updated on or after July 25, 2026. Older pages are being consolidated or removed from the public index. If we find fabricated sourcing, false testing claims, copied expression, or content that cannot stand on its evidence, we will correct, relabel, de-index, rewrite, or remove it.
  </p>

  <h2>Ask about our process</h2>
  <p>
    If a page leaves you unsure how AI was used, contact <a href={`mailto:${siteConfig.monetization.contactEmail}?subject=AI%20use%20question`}>{siteConfig.monetization.contactEmail}</a> with the URL and your question.
  </p>
</TransparencyPage>
